From 9778500b6b903b523fb48a434647297d648f9400 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 21:22:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(env): top-level launches strip the whole agent-scope env
 family, one shared list [daveey]

Hermes.app pid 59331 (reopened by a kanban worker's `hermes update`) carried not only
HERMES_DELEGATED_CHILD_CONTEXT + HERMES_KANBAN_* (already stripped since 4eff2691da) but
HERMES_SESSION_ID/SOURCE, HERMES_SINGLE_QUERY_SESSION and HERMES_SUPERVISED_CHILD, which
leak the worker's session identity and supervision mode into every Desktop backend.

The list now lives in agent/delegation_context.py (strip_agent_scope_env, next to
scrub_kanban_env): exact keys HERMES_DELEGATED_CHILD_CONTEXT / SINGLE_QUERY_SESSION / SPAWN /
SUPERVISED_CHILD plus the HERMES_KANBAN_/SESSION_/CRON_/PARENT_/RPC_/KERNEL_ prefixes,
keeping operator tunables (*_TIMEOUT, *_SECONDS, *_MAX_PARALLEL...). Still gated on a worker
marker so a deliberate board pin is untouched. top_level_env (CLI entry for
serve/dashboard/desktop/gui/gateway and the post-update Desktop reopen) uses it; the Electron
main mirror is drift-tested against it.

Why the worker shell had the marker at all: delegated_child_subprocess_env() adds
HERMES_DELEGATED_CHILD_CONTEXT to every subprocess a dispatcher worker spawns (terminal,
execute_code, MCP, LSP) by design, so its descendants can't mutate the board. A
`hermes update` in a worker's terminal therefore always carries it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
